These are the eight kinds of happiness of a recluse (samaṇa-sukha) that can be attained and provided. Furthermore, this hermitage is of a kind that can support the development of the contemplation of impermanence (aniccānupassanā) and the contemplation of suffering (dukkhānupassanā), enabling one to carry the strength of insight (vipassanā) so that the direct knowledges (abhiññā) and attainments (samāpatti) may be achieved without difficulty by those residing in the leaf-hut and park.

When the celestial architect Vissakamma created the hermitage for the Bodhisatta Sumedha the Wise to reside in, he fashioned a delightful park and leaf-hut adorned with pavilions, caves, and cloister paths. It was covered with various kinds of flowering and fruiting trees, making it pleasant and charming. It was complete with a pond of sweet, cool water. It was free from lions, leopards, and other wild beasts, as well as various ill-omened birds, allowing for a quiet and peaceful residence. At both ends of the cloister path, he prepared backrests (wooden slabs) to lean against. In the middle of the cloister path, for the Bodhisatta to sit upon, he arranged a smooth, clean, and level stone slab the color of green mung beans.

Inside the leaf-roofed hut, he also provided the requisites of an ascetic, such as matted hair, bark garments, a wooden tripod, and a water jug.

