In this context, the word uposatha has [five meanings]:
(1) The meaning of the recitation of the Pātimokkha by monks;
(2) The meaning of a name given for easy reference;
(3) The meaning of practice (upavāsa);
(4) The meaning of the virtue to be practiced;
(5) The meaning of the day on which the virtue is practiced.
Among these five meanings, the first pertains only to monks. The second is merely a name for reference, such as Uposatha Prince or Uposatha the Elephant King, and is not relevant to this section on virtue. Only the last three meanings are relevant here.

Among those last three, the meaning of 'upavāsa' (practice) is to live while fulfilling [the rules]. It means living while fulfilling the training rules. The fourth meaning, 'the virtue to be practiced,' means the virtue that should be lived while being fulfilled. The fifth meaning, 'the day on which the virtue is practiced,' means the day on which one lives while fulfilling the virtue; that very day is called the Uposatha day (uposatha-ne).

Regarding the five and ten precepts, ancient virtuous people did not usually practice them by designating specific days. However, since they were accustomed to practicing these eight precepts by designating specific days, it should be understood that the special addition "uposathaṅgavasena" (by way of the Uposatha factors) is used only for these eight precepts.

