In the Cariyāpiṭaka Commentary, it is explained in detail according to the first meaning. (This is clearly shown in the previous Miscellaneous Section.) (Alternatively) Although the wise Sumedha and the Hatthipāla brothers first went forth to the forest and only later became monks, it can be said that the state of monkhood to be attained later was the primary cause, as they went forth only because they wished to become monks. (Just as in the statement 'cutting wood to build a palace,' although cutting the wood happens first, the wood is cut because of the desire to build a palace; thus, the desire to build the palace is the cause, and cutting the wood is the effect.)

## Five Types of Forest Dwelling
There are five types of forest dwelling:
(1) Dwelling in the forest because of dullness and stupidity, without knowing any cause or effect;
(2) Dwelling in the forest with evil desires, thinking, 'If I live in the forest, people will give me many offerings as a forest-dweller';
(3) Dwelling in the forest because of insanity;
(4) Dwelling in the forest because it is a practice praised by the virtuous ones such as the Buddha;
(5) Dwelling in the forest based on noble qualities such as fewness of wishes and contentment.

The Buddha taught these five types of forest dwelling in the Vinaya Parivāra Pāḷi, Ekuttarikanaya Pañcaka, and the Upālipañhā Dhutaṅgavagga (Vin. 5, 233 and 335). Among these five types of forest dwelling (renunciation), the latter two are to be understood as praiseworthy forest dwelling (renunciation).
